{'id':'stats','title':'Statistics, metrics & data quality','sub':'Keep the denominator, uncertainty and business question visible.','rows':[
 ['Mean / median','Mean is sensitive to outliers; median is useful for skewed values.'],['Weighted mean','SUM(value × weight) / SUM(weight); validate the weights.'],['Percentage change','(new − old) / old; format as a percentage and define what happens at old = 0.'],['Percentage points','30% − 20% = 10 percentage points; the relative increase is 50%.'],['Conversion rate','Conversions / eligible opportunities over a defined period.'],['IQR','Q3 − Q1; inspect the distribution before using outlier rules.'],['Sample / population spread','Use STDEV.S for sample estimation; STDEV.P for the full population described.'],['Correlation','Association is not evidence of causation.']],
'code':'BEFORE ANALYSIS\nQuestion → audience → metric → grain → period\n\nAFTER EVERY TRANSFORMATION\nRows → keys → missingness → totals → exceptions\n\nBEFORE SHARING\nSource → assumptions → limits → privacy → owner',
'notes':['A p-value is not the probability that the null hypothesis is true. Explain the test assumptions, effect size and uncertainty instead of treating 0.05 as an automatic business decision.','A frequentist 95% confidence procedure covers the fixed true parameter in 95% of repeated samples under its assumptions; it is not a guarantee about an individual outcome.','Missing is not automatically zero. Investigate causes and document any exclusion or imputation. Reconcile with an independent method where possible.','Use only permitted public/synthetic practice data. Minimise personal information, keep raw data unchanged and document transformations and retention.'],
'links':[('UK data-protection principles','https://ico.org.uk/for-organisations/uk-gdpr-guidance-and-resources/data-protection-principles/a-guide-to-the-data-protection-principles/')]},
